# ABK SoC Power Optimization
#
config ABK_SOC_OPT
	tristate "ABK SoC power optimization"
	depends on CPU_FREQ
	default y
	help
	  Kernel-side cpufreq capping module.
	  Uses a cpufreq policy notifier to enforce per-cluster
	  frequency limits, preventing userspace daemons (perfd,
	  thermal-engine) from overriding them.

	  Sysfs control at /sys/kernel/abk_soc_opt/
	  Works with the AppOpt userspace tool.

	  BUILD MODE:
	  Default is Y (builtin). This matches the project's existing CI
	  and Kleaf configuration (abk_control is bool/builtin too), and
	  builtin never goes through modpost, so it cannot hit
	  "undefined!" errors for symbols the kernel does not export.

	  The one caveat of builtin is initcall ordering: the driver runs
	  at device_initcall time, which on Qualcomm platforms is earlier
	  than qcom-cpufreq-hw finishes probing. cpufreq_cpu_get() then
	  returns NULL for every CPU and num_clusters stays 0. Since v2.4
	  the driver self-heals from this: it retries the scan, rescans on
	  the first CPUFREQ_CREATE_POLICY notification, and exposes
	  /sys/kernel/abk_soc_opt/scan for a manual rescan.

	  M (loadable module) is supported too and is easier to debug
	  (rmmod/modprobe without a reboot), but Kleaf then requires the
	  .ko to be listed in kernel_aarch64's module_outs attribute or the
	  build fails with "built but not copied". Enable with:
	      ABK_SOC_OPT_MODULE=m ./setup.sh
	  and add to common/BUILD.bazel:
	      module_outs = ["drivers/abk_soc_opt/abk_soc_opt.ko"]
